Q4. Network Capacity Analysis
1. Usable IP addresses in original 192.168.10.0/24: 254 usable IPs (256 - 2).
2. Can all five departments fit into 192.168.10.0/24? 
   No. Total capacity required across all departments including 20% growth is 207 IPs. However, due to IP block sizing rules (powers of 2), allocating blocks requires 128 + 64 + 64 + 32 + 32 = 320 IP addresses. Thus, a single /24 prefix is insufficient.
3. Recommended Alternative Plan:
   Upgrade to a /23 network prefix (e.g., 192.168.10.0/23, providing 512 IPs) or use a 10.0.0.0/16 private block.
   Using 192.168.10.0/23 allocation:
   - Development Team: 192.168.10.0/25 (192.168.10.1 - 192.168.10.126)
   - SOC & IT Ops: 192.168.10.128/26 (192.168.10.129 - 192.168.10.190)
   - HR & Finance: 192.168.10.192/26 (192.168.10.193 - 192.168.10.254)
   - Reception & Admin: 192.168.11.0/27 (192.168.11.1 - 192.168.11.30)
   - Server Room: 192.168.11.32/27 (192.168.11.33 - 192.168.11.62)
4. Why Plan for Future Growth?
   Planning prevents IP address exhaustion, reduces network redesign downtime, avoids re-IPing devices, and allows seamless addition of new equipment and VLANs.

